The new `titleContent?: ReactNode` prop is a named `ReactNode` slot whose sole purpose is to render UI in the title position — the component just renders it via `{titleContent ?? (!!title && <Text>...)}`. This is configuration disguised as a slot: every new title variant has to be threaded through `WidgetContainer`'s prop interface, and `titleContent` now competes with the existing `title` prop (an either/or the component has to arbitrate). Per Case 2, a named `ReactNode` optional prop that only exists to fill a specific position should be a composable child instead.

Expose the title area as a compound-component slot so consumers compose it directly, e.g.:

```tsx
// WidgetContainer.tsx
function Title({children}: {children: ReactNode}) {
    const styles = useThemeStyles();
    const theme = useTheme();
    return <Text style={styles.getWidgetContainerTitleStyle(theme.text)}>{children}</Text>;
}
WidgetContainer.Title = Title;

// Consumer (InsightsSectionContent.tsx)
<WidgetContainer>
    <WidgetContainer.Title>
        <InsightTitleDropdown configs={dropdownConfigs} selectedKey={config.key} onSelect={onSelectInsight} />
    </WidgetContainer.Title>
    ...
</WidgetContainer>
```

This keeps `WidgetContainer` stable — a plain-text title and a dropdown title are both just children, with no new prop and no `titleContent ?? title` branching inside the component.

This adds a named `ReactNode` slot prop (`rowFooter`) to the shared, compound `TableRow` component whose sole purpose is to render `{rowFooter}` below the row grid (see line 256). This is configuration via a positional slot prop: every consumer that wants different below-the-row content now depends on `TableRow` exposing this exact slot, and adding another slot later means modifying `TableRow`'s props and internals again. `Table.Row` already accepts a render-prop `children`, which is the composition foundation.

Instead of adding a slot prop, let the consumer compose the footer inside the row's existing `children`:

```tsx
<Table.Row interactive rowIndex={rowIndex} /* ...no rowFooter prop... */>
    {({hovered}) => (
        <>
            {/* existing row cells */}
            {frozenByRowFooter}
        </>
    )}
</Table.Row>
```

This keeps the shared `TableRow` stable and lets consumers own what renders below the grid.

The `modal` prop is a named `React.JSX.Element` slot rendered positionally as `{modal}`. This is configuration disguised as flexibility — the layout owns a fixed slot the consumer fills via a prop, so any new sibling element forces a new slot prop on this interface. Slots like this should be composable children the consumer places directly.

Since the layout already accepts `children`, render the long-press menu as a child alongside the list instead of threading it through a dedicated prop:

```tsx
// SearchListViewLayout: drop the `modal` prop, just render children
<View style={[styles.flex1, !isKeyboardShown && safeAreaPaddingBottomStyle, containerStyle]}>
    {children}
</View>

// ExpenseFlatSearchView passes both the list and the menu as children
<SearchListViewLayout ...>
    <BaseSearchList ... />
    {modal}
</SearchListViewLayout>
```

The new `selectionTopBar` prop is configuration rather than composition: it is an optional prop whose sole purpose is the conditional render `{!!selectionTopBar && <SelectionTopBar {...selectionTopBar} />}`. The consumer assembles a config object that the layout spreads back into a child it owns. Adding/altering the header bar requires changing this component's API and internals, and the layout must know about `SelectionTopBar` specifically.

Prefer exposing the header as a composable child so the view renders its own `SelectionTopBar` and the layout never needs to know about it:

```tsx
// SearchListViewLayout — generic header slot, no SelectionTopBar coupling
<View style={[styles.flex1, !isKeyboardShown && safeAreaPaddingBottomStyle, containerStyle]}>
    {header}
    {children}
    {modal}
</View>

// ExpenseFlatSearchView — owns its header
<SearchListViewLayout ...>
    {tableHeaderVisible && (
        <SelectionTopBar isLargeScreenWidth={isLargeScreenWidth} canSelectMultiple={canSelectMultiple} ... />
    )}
    <BaseSearchList ... />
</SearchListViewLayout>
```

This change replaces the `stateSelectorRoute` config prop with a boolean flag `isMoneyRequestDynamicStateSelector` that the component branches on inside `onPress` to decide which route to navigate to. A boolean flag that selects between behaviors inside the component is configuration (Case 1): every new navigation target the selector must support will require another flag and another branch, growing the component's API and internals.

Prefer exposing an abstract capability rather than a behavior flag. For example, let the consumer own where navigation goes by passing an abstract handler, and have the component report the selected value:

```tsx
type StateSelectorProps = {
    /** Called when the user opens the state picker, with the current state code */
    onOpenStateSelector?: (stateCode?: string, label?: string) => void;
    // ...
};

// onPress:
onPress={() => {
    didOpenStateSelector.current = true;
    onOpenStateSelector?.(stateCode, label);
}}
```

The consumer (`PaymentCardForm`) then decides whether to navigate to the dynamic money-request route or the settings address-state route, and `StateSelector` no longer needs to know about either.

`renderReconciliationAccountSettings` is a render function prop (Case 5). The parent component `DynamicReconciliationAccountSettingsPage` defines a function that returns a full JSX tree (`ConnectionLayout` with `SelectionList`, `Text`, `RenderHTML`) and passes it as a prop to `ExpensifyCardDynamicReconciliation` and `TravelInvoicingDynamicReconciliation`. Each child calls this function to fill its UI. This couples the parent to every child's rendering needs and prevents React Compiler from independently memoizing the render output.

Instead, extract a shared `ReconciliationAccountSettingsLayout` component that accepts `description`, `html`, `selectedBankAccountID`, and `onSelectBankAccount` as regular props and renders the `ConnectionLayout` + `SelectionList` itself. Each child component would then render this layout component directly rather than calling a render function:

```tsx
function ReconciliationAccountSettingsLayout({description, html, selectedBankAccountID, onSelectBankAccount, ...layoutProps}) {
    return (
        <ConnectionLayout {...layoutProps}>
            <Text>{description}</Text>
            <RenderHTML html={html} />
            <SelectionList data={options} onSelectRow={({value}) => onSelectBankAccount(value)} />
        </ConnectionLayout>
    );
}
```

A single statically-known item is encoded as a config array (`sections`) and rendered via `.map()`. With only one item, this adds unnecessary indirection. The item should be rendered directly as JSX instead of going through a config-array pattern.

Replace the `sections` array + `.map()` with direct JSX:

```tsx
<OfflineWithFeedback
    pendingAction={settingsPendingAction(payableAccountSetting, config?.pendingFields)}
    key={payableAccountSetting.at(0)}
    errorRowStyles={[styles.ph5]}
>
    <MenuItemWithTopDescription
        title={travelPayableAccount?.name}
        description={translate('workspace.common.travelInvoicingPayableAccount')}
        onPress={() => {
            if (!policyID) {
                return;
            }
            Navigation.navigate(createDynamicRoute(DYNAMIC_ROUTES.POLICY_ACCOUNTING_XERO_TRAVEL_INVOICING_PAYABLE_ACCOUNT_SELECT.path, travelInvoicingPath));
        }}
        shouldShowRightIcon
        brickRoadIndicator={areSettingsInErrorFields(payableAccountSetting, config?.errorFields) ? CONST.BRICK_ROAD_INDICATOR_STATUS.ERROR : undefined}
    />
</OfflineWithFeedback>
```

This removes the `XeroSectionType` type definition and the `sections` array, simplifying the component.

The `shouldHideHeader` boolean flag controls whether `HeaderWithBackButton` renders via `{!shouldHideHeader && (<HeaderWithBackButton .../>)}`. This is a configuration-driven pattern where a boolean prop toggles the presence of a UI feature. The caller (`IOURequestStartPage`) already knows it does not want a header, so composition is the better fit: the parent should own the header and simply not render it, rather than the child accepting a flag to hide it.

Consider splitting `IOURequestStepConfirmation` so the content (without the header) is a standalone component that `IOURequestStartPage` can embed directly, while the existing route-based entry point wraps it with the header. For example:

```tsx
// IOURequestStepConfirmationContent — no header, embeddable
function IOURequestStepConfirmationContent(props) { /* current body minus header */ }

// Route entry — adds the header
function IOURequestStepConfirmation(props) {
    return (
        <>
            <HeaderWithBackButton ... />
            <IOURequestStepConfirmationContent {...props} />
        </>
    );
}
```

The getting started items are a finite, statically-known set of onboarding steps (createWorkspace, connectAccounting/customizeCategories, linkCompanyCards, setupRules), each with distinct routes and completion logic. Building them as a config array in `useGettingStartedItems` and mapping them through a generic `GettingStartedRow` component is a Case 4 violation (config-array driven rendering). Business logic (conditional inclusion, completion checks) is embedded in the data declarations rather than expressed as declarative JSX.

Declare each item as explicit JSX with conditional rendering instead of mapping over a config array:

```tsx
function GettingStartedSection() {
    // ... hooks ...
    return (
        <WidgetContainer title={translate('homePage.gettingStartedSection.title')}>
            <View style={containerStyle}>
                <GettingStartedRow
                    label={translate('homePage.gettingStartedSection.createWorkspace')}
                    isComplete
                    route={ROUTES.WORKSPACE_OVERVIEW.getRoute(activePolicyID)}
                />
                {isDirectConnect ? (
                    <GettingStartedRow
                        label={translate('homePage.gettingStartedSection.connectAccounting', {integrationName})}
                        isComplete={hasAccountingConnections(policy)}
                        route={ROUTES.WORKSPACE_ACCOUNTING.getRoute(activePolicyID)}
                    />
                ) : (
                    <GettingStartedRow
                        label={translate('homePage.gettingStartedSection.customizeCategories')}
                        isComplete={hasCustomCategories(policyCategories)}
                        route={ROUTES.WORKSPACE_CATEGORIES.getRoute(activePolicyID)}
                    />
                )}
                {policy.areCompanyCardsEnabled && (
                    <GettingStartedRow ... />
                )}
                {policy.areRulesEnabled && (
                    <GettingStartedRow ... />
                )}
            </View>
        </WidgetContainer>
    );
}
```

This makes the render tree visible, removes the intermediary config array, and lets each row be independently modifiable.

