The `sections` array encodes 2 statically-known items as a config array that is `.map()`'d to produce JSX (Case 4: config-array driven rendering). With only 2 fixed items, each with distinct navigation behavior, this would be clearer as inline JSX.

Instead of building a config array and mapping it, render each section directly:

```tsx
<ConnectionLayout ...>
    <OfflineWithFeedback
        pendingAction={settingsPendingAction(vendor, qboConfig?.pendingFields)}
        key="vendor"
        errorRowStyles={[styles.ph5]}
    >
        <MenuItemWithTopDescription
            title={travelVendor?.name}
            description={translate('workspace.qbo.travelInvoicingVendor')}
            onPress={() => { ... }}
            shouldShowRightIcon
            brickRoadIndicator={...}
        />
    </OfflineWithFeedback>
    <OfflineWithFeedback
        pendingAction={settingsPendingAction(payableAccount, qboConfig?.pendingFields)}
        key="payableAccount"
        errorRowStyles={[styles.ph5]}
    >
        <MenuItemWithTopDescription
            title={travelPayableAccount?.name}
            description={translate('workspace.qbo.travelInvoicingPayableAccount')}
            onPress={() => { ... }}
            shouldShowRightIcon
            brickRoadIndicator={...}
        />
    </OfflineWithFeedback>
</ConnectionLayout>
```

This also eliminates the need for the `QBOSectionType` type definition and the `eslint-disable` for `react/no-array-index-key`.

